A||N||5||kinboyatuwo||Control arm bushings!!!||||5||
Z||000000||kinboyatuwo||04-30-2001||02:24 PM||||Well i am sure the uppers on both sides are shot but how hard are they all to change???<P>i am going to do it for sure and want to kknow if i need any special tools??<P>loks like it shouldnt but would like to know BEFORE it is apart<P>thanks guys<BR>chris<P>------------------<BR>"Friends don't let friends drive four doors."<P>87 Duke, 235,000km||||reg||5
Z||000001||Tigger||04-30-2001||04:39 PM||||Shouldn't need any special tools other than a pickle fork or ball joint seperator tool.  For the bushings you can knock out the center sleeve and then torch out the rubber.  I just did mine and after the inner sleeve was out, I could pull and twist one of the bushings out by hand because they were VERY worn.<P>I remember hearing someone on the forum talk about drilling out the rubber in several places and then pull chunks of it out.  Never tried that myself, could be something worth trying.<P>Good advise would be to follow the Haynes manual on removing the upper control arm, you will need to support the lower arm with a jack. and the manual has the torque specs to bolt everything back together.<P>BTW are you planning on replacing them with poly?<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 04-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||kinboyatuwo||04-30-2001||05:41 PM||||thanks for a start<BR>i have the haynes manual and will follow it word for word. <P>i am not sure if i will go with poly, i am in canada and am afraid of exchange and duties.<P>i think all i can get up here will be the rubber, anyone out there know??<P>------------------<BR>"Friends don't let friends drive four doors."<P>87 Duke, 235,000km||||reg||3
Z||000003||Bob Englert||04-30-2001||06:19 PM||||Upper control arm bushings should be available from AutoZone or any dealer that sells Moog suspension parts.<P>If you are replacing them with rubber bushings, you will need to press out the rubber and the metal sleeve.  You will probably need a press to do this.  When I have rebuilt the suspension in 3 of my Fiero's, I removed the control arms and took them to an auto machine shop.  They can press out the old and press in the new ones very cheaply.  <P>If you are using poly bushings just burn then out as others have said.<P>I would recommend Rodney Dickman's ball joint separating tool for the job.  Much easier than a pickle fork and not destructive to the boot or ball joint.  You might want to go ahead and replace the upper ball joints while they are apart.<P>I would also check the lower bushings and ball joints carefully when you get things apart.  The lowers can be a pain to remove due to the front springs, but if it was me I would do them all (ball joints and bushings) one time while everything was apart.  You will be amazed at how much better the car will feel.<P>Good luck...<P><BR>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl<p>[This message has been edited by Bob Englert (edited 04-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Eric||04-30-2001||06:30 PM||||I just burned mine out.  The center sleeve will just pop out on its own, and then you just keep burning the bushing until its center is gone.  After you put the fire out, use a screwdriver or a stick and pry out the rest of the bushing.  It's fun  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||PBJ||04-30-2001||10:44 PM||||Hey, I have done a few before. I didn't have any advise,or torches. just a pickle fork and a couple of hamers.  check the upper ball joints too-while your there.  mind you torches would be a great help.  carefull when un bolting upper control arm-be sure spring and lower control arm supported.||||reg||1
